[claims]

## (root)
#c1[i5] The anyhow library provides a trait object based error type called anyhow::Error for idiomatic error handling in Rust applications.
  > README.md:9-10 #332995edd222
#c2[i4] The anyhow::Result<T> type is an alias for Result<T, anyhow::Error> and is recommended as the return type for fallible functions.
  > README.md:23-24 #150385c39c7f
#c3[i3] Anyhow supports downcasting by value, shared reference, or mutable reference.
  > README.md:66-67 #e41e0e6ce253
#c4[i3] Setting RUST_LIB_BACKTRACE=1 enables backtraces specifically for errors, while RUST_BACKTRACE=1 enables them for both panics and errors.
  > README.md:85 #e2e2fa1521f8
#c5[i4] The anyhow! macro creates one-off error messages with string interpolation, and the bail! macro provides a shorthand for early returns.
  > README.md:111-112 #f47f319312be
#c6[i4] In no_std mode, the 'std' feature must be disabled in Cargo.toml and a global allocator is required.
  > README.md::"No-std support" #c52f3dc8d20d
#c7[i5] Anyhow is intended for application code where easy error handling is prioritized, whereas thiserror is for designing dedicated library error types.
  > README.md::"Comparison to thiserror" #6fd2d02da2d2

## src
#c8[i5] The Backtrace type is an alias for std::backtrace::Backtrace when the std feature is enabled, otherwise it is an empty enum.
  > src/backtrace.rs:2 #6160332ee130
#c9[i4] The backtrace! macro captures a standard backtrace if the std feature is enabled, returning Some(std::backtrace::Backtrace::capture()).
  > src/backtrace.rs:10 #d945ce0779d4
#c10[i4] When the std feature is disabled, the backtrace! macro always returns None.
  > src/backtrace.rs:17 #8da9f519eca1
#c11[i5] If error_generic_member_access is enabled, backtrace_if_absent checks if the error already contains a backtrace before capturing a new one.
  > src/backtrace.rs:24-27 #1937b53ed61c
#c12[i3] Without error_generic_member_access but with the std feature, backtrace_if_absent unconditionally attempts to capture a backtrace.
  > src/backtrace.rs:26 #de4432aeea80
#c13[i3] The Backtrace type is statically asserted to implement both Send and Sync traits.
  > src/backtrace.rs::_assert_send_sync #5f81a69db8b9
#c14[i5] The Chain struct provides an iterator over a sequence of errors, starting from a head error and following its source chain.
  > src/chain.rs::Chain #af852a4e439f
#c15[i4] ChainState uses a Linked variant for lazy traversal via error sources and a Buffered variant for pre-collected error sequences.
  > src/chain.rs::ChainState #a5a153eebc6b
#c16[i5] The Iterator implementation for Chain traverses the error hierarchy by calling error.source() on each step in the Linked state.
  > src/chain.rs::next #2da112204c77
#c17[i4] Calling next_back on a Linked Chain triggers a side effect that exhausts the source chain into a Vec and transitions the state to Buffered.
  > src/chain.rs::DoubleEndedIterator.next_back #95c585d2e85c
#c18[i3] The Chain iterator reports an exact size by traversing the remaining source chain to count elements.
  > src/chain.rs::ExactSizeIterator.len #0a50876f32c7
#c19[i3] The Buffered state and DoubleEndedIterator implementation are conditionally compiled based on the std feature or the absence of anyhow_no_core_error.
  > src/chain.rs::ChainState #a5a153eebc6b
#c20[i4] A new Chain is initialized in the Linked state with the provided head error as the first element.
  > src/chain.rs::new #87c2ee6783ff
#c21[i5] The Context trait is implemented for Result<T, E> where E implements the internal ext::StdError trait, allowing the attachment of context to errors.
  > src/context.rs:42-45 #9fed42fb4d16
#c22[i4] Implementations of context and with_context use explicit match statements instead of map_err or ok_or_else to avoid adding extra frames to captured backtraces.
  > src/context.rs::context #f36af7f6ccb8
#c23[i4] ContextError wraps a context and an error, implementing Display by showing only the context and source to return the underlying error.
  > src/context.rs:128-135 #596477ecfe4a
#c24[i3] The Debug implementation for ContextError uses a Quoted wrapper to escape the context string and labels the underlying error as 'source'.
  > src/context.rs::fmt #72ff97c1a9b3
#c25[i3] When error_generic_member_access is enabled, ContextError delegates the provide method to the underlying error's implementation.
  > src/context.rs:146-149 #a2a8e22c4710
#c26[i2] The Quoted struct ensures that context strings are wrapped in double quotes and debug-escaped during formatting.
  > src/context.rs:168-178 #3368701a1d35
#c27[i4] The BothDebug trait provides a method to dispatch error creation by rendering two Debug-implementing values into a message.
  > src/ensure.rs::__dispatch_ensure #55435802d61b
#c28[i4] The NotBothDebug trait serves as a fallback that returns a plain Error message when values do not both implement Debug.
  > src/ensure.rs:29-33 #e2b0c2c27232
#c29[i3] The Buf struct uses a fixed-size 40-byte array of MaybeUninit<u8> for stack-based string formatting.
  > src/ensure.rs::Buf #4a5b381c47a4
#c30[i3] Buf::write_str rejects strings containing spaces or newlines and returns fmt::Error if the input exceeds remaining capacity.
  > src/ensure.rs::Write.write_str #bd9dd04c4ac4
#c31[i5] The render function attempts to format two Debug values into a message; if formatting fails or exceeds Buf capacity, it returns the original message.
  > src/ensure.rs:83-85 #e2a3e1eb458c
#c32[i5] The __parse_ensure macro implements a recursive descent parser for Rust expressions to support 'fancy' ensure assertions.
  > src/ensure.rs:105-108 #917c66a62c94
#c33[i3] Low-precedence control flow constructs like return, break, and continue trigger a fallback ensure mechanism.
  > src/ensure.rs:112-114 #76d586786a64
#c34[i4] The macro parser handles complex Rust syntax including qualified paths, generics, and various pointer types like &raw mut.
  > src/ensure.rs:158-160 #0547e5cd245d
#c35[i5] The `Error` type is a thin pointer to an `ErrorImpl` structure, erasing the concrete error type `E` while preserving behavior via a custom vtable.
  > src/error.rs::Error.construct #018b71bbe165
#c36[i4] Creating a new `Error` via `Error::new` requires the error type to be `StdError + Send + Sync + 'static` and automatically captures a backtrace if one is absent.
  > src/error.rs::Error.new #e23a67bf8e65
#c37[i3] The `Error::msg` method creates an error from printable messages using `MessageError`, which is `repr(transparent)` to allow casting between the wrapper and the message.
  > src/error.rs::Error.msg #f3a6a5f5f2f1
#c38[i3] The `Error::chain` method provides an iterator over the cause chain, starting from the error the object was created from.
  > src/error.rs::Error.chain #a481f44c167b
#c39[i3] Backtrace availability depends on `RUST_LIB_BACKTRACE` or `RUST_BACKTRACE` environment variables; `RUST_LIB_BACKTRACE=1` enables them specifically for errors.
  > src/error.rs:387-390 #05511d70cab1
#c40[i3] The `Error::root_cause` method returns the final `StdError` in the chain, which is the lowest level cause.
  > src/error.rs::Error.root_cause #8321e6ef8956
#c41[i3] The `ErrorImpl` struct uses `repr(C)` to ensure the error object `E` remains in the final position, facilitating type erasure and vtable-based manipulation.
  > src/error.rs::Some #bf9085671ef1
#c42[i3] The `vtable` function retrieves the `ErrorVTable` by assuming it is the first field of the `ErrorImpl` struct.
  > src/error.rs::vtable #2a024b6ce718
#c43[i2] The `Error` type implements `UnwindSafe` and `RefUnwindSafe` traits.
  > src/error.rs:1059-1060 #1fed0d0ee956

[probes]
?p1 Which environment variable should be set to enable backtraces for errors only?
  = RUST_LIB_BACKTRACE=1
  > README.md:85 #e2e2fa1521f8
?p2 What does the backtrace! macro return when the std feature is not enabled?
  = None
  > src/backtrace.rs:17 #8da9f519eca1
?p3 What happens to the internal state of a Linked Chain when next_back() is called?
  = It collects all remaining errors into a Vec and transitions the state to Buffered.
  > src/chain.rs::DoubleEndedIterator.next_back #95c585d2e85c
?p4 Why does the implementation of context for Result use a match statement instead of map_err?
  = To save 2 useless frames off the captured backtrace.
  > src/context.rs::context #f36af7f6ccb8
?p5 What is the maximum capacity of the internal formatting buffer used during error rendering?
  = 40 bytes
  > src/ensure.rs::Buf #4a5b381c47a4
?p6 What trait bounds are required for a type to be used with Error::new?
  = The type must implement StdError + Send + Sync + 'static.
  > src/error.rs::Error.new #e23a67bf8e65
?p7 What assumption does the `vtable` function make about the layout of `ErrorImpl`?
  = It assumes that `ErrorVTable` is the first field of the `ErrorImpl` struct.
  > src/error.rs::vtable #2a024b6ce718
?p8 What specific requirement must be met to use Anyhow in no_std mode?
  = A global allocator is required.
  > README.md::"No-std support" #c52f3dc8d20d
?p9 Which function is used to check for an existing backtrace in an error when error_generic_member_access is active?
  = $crate::nightly::request_ref_backtrace
  > src/backtrace.rs:24 #d53b93d0d758
?p10 How does the Chain iterator determine the next error in a Linked state?
  = It calls the source() method on the current error.
  > src/chain.rs::next #2da112204c77
?p11 What does the Display implementation of ContextError output?
  = It outputs the context field using its Display implementation.
  > src/context.rs:133 #5ea9ce45e221
?p12 What characters cause Buf::write_str to return an error regardless of remaining capacity?
  = Spaces (' ') or newlines ('\n')
  > src/ensure.rs::Write.write_str #bd9dd04c4ac4
?p13 Which environment variable configuration enables backtraces for errors but not for panics?
  = Setting RUST_LIB_BACKTRACE=1 and RUST_BACKTRACE=0 (or leaving RUST_BACKTRACE unset while setting RUST_LIB_BACKTRACE=1).
  > src/error.rs:396-397 #0266d56d22a4
?p14 What traits must the Backtrace type satisfy according to the internal assertions?
  = Send and Sync
  > src/backtrace.rs::_assert_send_sync.assert #c40ef0856c1b
?p15 Under what configuration is the Default trait implemented for Chain?
  = When the 'std' feature is enabled or anyhow_no_core_error is not set.
  > src/chain.rs:93-94 #768fa202e942
?p16 How is the source of a ContextError<C, Error> retrieved?
  = It uses an unsafe call to crate::ErrorImpl::error on the inner error reference.
  > src/context.rs:157 #5854e0dee432
?p17 How does the render function format the final error message when both sides are successfully buffered?
  = It combines the message, lhs, and rhs in the format "{msg} ({lhs} vs {rhs})"
  > src/ensure.rs:91-96 #e3a28df2d215
